<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Las Médulas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1st–3rd century</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Las Médulas is a former Roman gold-mining area near Ponferrada in the province of León, in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. From the 1st century CE Roman engineers brought water through long canals and used it to collapse whole hillsides, a method <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a> called ruina montium. The eroded red landscape left behind has been a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> since 1997.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/las-medulas.jpg" alt="Las Médulas" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rafael Ibáñez Fernández · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panor%C3%A1mica_de_Las_M%C3%A9dulas.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
